   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Mapping the Results\n",
    "A table is useful, but this map divusally demonstrates the park coverage! \n",
    "(Darker green neighborhoods have a higher percentage of parkland.)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 82,
   "id": "556ccf8c-f78a-49b4-bad6-cfbaedbc1878",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1000x800 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Create a plot for the neighborhoods.\n",
    "nhPlot = neighborhoods.plot(\n",
    "    column=\"coverage\",\n",
    "    legend=True,\n",
    "    cmap=\"Greens\",\n",
    "    edgecolor=\"black\",\n",
    "    figsize=(10, 8)\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Get the top 10 neighborhoods, ranked by coverage.\n",
    "top10 = neighborhoods.sort_values(\"coverage\", ascending=False).head(10)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Add labels to the top 10 neighborhoods!\n",
    "for rank, (_, row) in enumerate(top10.iterrows(), start=1):\n",
    "    point = row.geometry.representative_point()\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Give the top three special colors and larger labels.\n",
    "    colors = {1: \"red\", 2: \"crimson\", 3: \"firebrick\"}\n",
    "    labelColor = colors.get(rank, \"black\")\n",
    "    labelSize = 14 if rank <= 3 else 10\n",
    "\n",
    "    nhPlot.annotate(\n",
    "        text=f\"{rank}. {row['hood']}\",\n",
    "        xy=(point.x, point.y),\n",
    "        ha=\"center\",\n",
    "        color=labelColor,\n",
    "        fontsize=labelSize\n",
    "    )\n",
    "\n",
    "nhPlot.set_axis_off()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "483df0d5-967d-4c73-bd6b-782a2e93af09",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Interpreting the Results\n",
    "\n",
    "* ### Analyzing the data the data reveals that Pittsburgh's parks are not evenly distributed across its neighborhoods. While some neighborhoods contain far larger acreage of park land, others have relatively less in comparison, implying that opportunities to enjoy green spaces may vary depending on where you live in the nighborhood.\n",
    "\n",
    "* ### By measuring park land as a percentage of each neighborhood's total area, we can successfully compare neighborhoods of different sizes rather than simply favoring those with the largest parks.\n",
    "\n",
    "* ### Despite this, park coverage alone does *not* fully determine how enjoyable/accessible a neighborhood is. **Basing a neighborhood on green space alone is inaccurate!**\n",
    "\n",
    "    * ### My specific submetric measures the amount of park land, but there's other data left unaccounted for (such as the previously-listed measure of accessibility). These limitations show through the analyses of this project's additional metrics of recreational and quality of life."
   ]
  }
